window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][40] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A carrier sends &quot;delivered&quot; and then, a minute later, &quot;out for delivery.&quot; What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Treat the second event as out of order or a mistake: keep the order in 'delivered' because the state machine does not go backward without an explicit exception, but record the odd event, and flag the order for review if the customer reports non-receipt. The timeline can show both with timestamps.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle a carrier with no electronic updates? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Poll its public tracking where permitted, use scans at our own facilities, accept less frequent updates and say so ('updates may be delayed'), and push the carrier toward a feed in the contract. The customer's view is honest about the data's freshness.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c22-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C22-08 · Hard · system design · SD · Netflix</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design how a streaming service starts playing a video quickly and keeps playing smoothly.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Stream the video from a server.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the content network, adaptive quality and buffering.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes startup time under different networks, explains starting low and ramping, covers failover and regional capacity, and sets measures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real design. The job: press play and see video within about two seconds, without stalls, on any connection.</p>\n<p><em>Requirements.</em> Time to first frame under about 2 seconds at the 95th percentile; very few stalls; quality as high as the connection allows; works on slow networks and old devices. Must not: show an endless spinner.</p>\n<p><em>Sketch.</em> Video is stored as many short segments (about 2 seconds each) at several quality levels. <em>Start:</em> the app asks the service to authorize playback and gets a manifest (a list of segments and quality levels); it fetches segments from a nearby server in a content delivery network, which holds popular titles close to viewers. <em>Adaptive quality:</em> the player measures its download speed and chooses the quality of each next segment, moving up when it can and down when it must.</p>\n<p><em>The startup arithmetic.</em> A 2-second segment at 3 Mbps is 0.75 MB. On a 10 Mbps connection it downloads in 0.6 seconds; on a 2 Mbps connection, in 3.0 seconds. If instead the player starts at a lower quality of 0.75 Mbps, the first segment is about 0.19 MB and downloads in 0.75 seconds on the 2 Mbps connection. So starting low and ramping up cuts startup on slow connections from about 3 seconds to under 1, at the cost of a few seconds of lower picture quality, which most viewers prefer to waiting.</p>\n<p><em>Capacity and placement.</em> Popular titles are copied to servers near viewers ahead of demand, predicted from what viewers watch; less popular titles are fetched on demand. Before a major release, copy the title to regions in advance and reserve capacity; stage availability by region if demand is uncertain (C22 CS-C22-02).</p>\n<p><em>Failure and degradation.</em> If the nearest server fails or is overloaded, the player switches to another source mid-stream; if bandwidth drops, the player lowers quality rather than stall; if the authorization service is slow, a short-lived cached permission lets recent viewers start. The viewer should see at worst a reduced picture, not a stall.</p>\n<p><em>Measures.</em> Time to first frame (median and 95th percentile), failed starts, rebuffering time per hour, average quality delivered, with guardrails on cost per hour streamed.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Netflix has published on its content delivery network and on resilience testing, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of playback and graceful-degradation prompts <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog on Open Connect and resilience testing; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's numbers or design.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you prepare for a huge release, say a season everyone has waited for? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Predict demand by region, pre-position the files near viewers before release, reserve network and server capacity, lower the starting quality briefly if needed, and watch the stall and failed-start rates live with a plan to shed quality, not viewers. A staged rollout by region helps if the forecast is uncertain.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why not always start at the highest quality? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The first segment at high quality is large; on a slow connection that is the long wait the viewer notices. Starting low and ramping up delivers a fast start and reaches high quality within seconds on a good connection.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c22-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C22-09 · Hard · system design · SD · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design webhook delivery for a payments platform so that merchants never miss an event.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Send an HTTP request to the merchant when something happens.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Covers retries, signatures, and duplicates.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains at-least-once delivery, backoff and its load effects, ordering, replay and catch-up, merchant-side idempotency, and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. The job: tell a merchant's system that something happened (a payment succeeded, a refund was issued) reliably, even when the merchant's server is slow or down.</p>\n<p><em>Requirements.</em> Every event is delivered at least once; most within seconds; no forged events; merchants can recover missed events; the platform is not overwhelmed by a merchant's outage. Non-goals: guaranteed ordering.</p>\n<p><em>Sketch.</em> When an event occurs, the platform records it in an event log with a unique identifier, then enqueues a delivery task. A delivery worker sends the event to the merchant's registered address, signed so the merchant can verify its origin, and expects a success response within a short time (say 10 seconds). On failure or timeout, the task is retried later according to a schedule.</p>\n<p><em>Retry schedule and the arithmetic.</em> Suppose 100 million events a day: about 1,160 a second. If 2% fail on the first attempt, that is 2 million retries needing a second attempt. A growing schedule (for example 1 minute, 5 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours, 12 hours, 24 hours: six attempts over a day) spreads the retry load. If a merchant's endpoint is down for an hour while receiving 1,160 events a second of platform traffic for it (a large merchant), about 4.2 million events accumulate; without backoff the platform would hammer a server that is already down, and with backoff and a per-merchant limit it does not.</p>\n<p><em>Duplicates and order.</em> Because delivery is at least once, a merchant can receive the same event twice; each event has an identifier, and merchants should record processed identifiers and ignore repeats. Events can arrive out of order; the platform tells merchants to treat the event as a notification and fetch the current state from the API if order matters.</p>\n<p><em>Replay and catch-up.</em> After a long outage, a merchant needs the events it missed: provide an API to list events from a given time and a dashboard button to resend. Mark endpoints that fail for days as disabled with an alert to the merchant.</p>\n<p><em>Security.</em> Sign each delivery with a secret and include a timestamp so that old messages cannot be replayed by an attacker; deliver only over secure connections.</p>\n<p><em>Failure and monitoring.</em> Merchants see delivery status and errors per event; the platform alerts on rising failure rates and on the oldest undelivered event. A slow endpoint (not down) is treated as a failure after the timeout and subject to a concurrency limit, so one slow merchant cannot tie up the workers.</p>\n<p><em>Measures.</em> Share of events delivered on the first attempt, time to delivery (95th percentile), events outstanding after 24 hours, merchant-reported missed events (target zero).</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe's documentation describes webhooks with retries and signatures, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of developer-delivery design prompts <code>[V: Stripe API documentation; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's internals.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if a merchant's endpoint is slow but not down? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Treat a response slower than the timeout as a failure and retry later, and cap how many deliveries are in flight to that endpoint so it cannot occupy all workers. Tell the merchant through the dashboard that its endpoint is slow, with the typical response time, so they can fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How can a merchant catch up after a three-day outage? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The event log is the source of truth: the merchant lists events since the outage began through the API and processes them, skipping identifiers it has seen. Retries stop after the schedule ends, so replay is the safety net.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c22-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C22-10 · Intermediate · system design · SD</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Explain the design of your notification system to the CEO in three minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A technical walk through the components.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Leads with the user outcome and the main risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the decision needed, cost and time in plain terms, explains the key tradeoff and the biggest risk with a mitigation, and offers a next step.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd lead with what matters to them and drop the boxes.</p>\n<p>&quot;<em>The outcome.</em> People get the right notification at the right time, once, on the channel they prefer, which brings them back without annoying them. Today we send some twice and some late.</p>\n<p><em>How it works, in one picture.</em> Events from the product go into one pipeline that checks each person's preferences, removes duplicates, groups bursts, and sends by push or email through our providers. If a provider is slow, messages wait and retry; important ones, like security alerts, go first.</p>\n<p><em>The numbers.</em> About 160 million notifications a day, with evening peaks about five times the average, around 9,000 a second. Our push provider's limits are the thing to watch at that level.</p>\n<p><em>The main tradeoff.</em> We can send instantly, or group messages for a few seconds to cut annoyance and cost. I recommend short grouping for messages and longer for likes.</p>\n<p><em>The biggest risk.</em> Spam and abuse: bots mass-mentioning people. We cap how many a person can receive from one sender and give users controls to mute and report.</p>\n<p><em>Cost and time.</em> The first version takes one team about a quarter; running cost is mostly provider fees, roughly proportional to volume, and grouping lowers it.</p>\n<p><em>What I need from you.</em> A decision to launch to 1% of users next month with guardrails on opt-outs and uninstalls, and agreement that security alerts get priority over everything else. I'll report weekly.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The CEO asks, &quot;Can we launch next month?&quot; <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A limited launch to 1% of users, yes. Full launch depends on two things we will know in four weeks: whether the provider's limits hold at our peak and whether opt-out rates stay flat. I will tell you the result on a date, and I would not promise the full launch before then.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The CEO asks, &quot;Why does this cost so much?&quot; <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The cost is mostly sending: every message to a phone or inbox has a fee, and we send 160 million a day. Grouping bursts and preferring push over email are the two levers; I can show you the cost per user per month and what each lever saves.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c22-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-01 · Estimate a chat app</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A chat app has 30 million daily users, each sending 40 messages a day, at 0.5 KB a message. Estimate the average and peak writes per second (peak factor 5), the daily storage and the storage for a year, and say what each number suggests.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A first estimate.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C22-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Messages a day: 30,000,000 × 40 = 1.2 billion; per second: 1,200,000,000 ÷ 86,400 = about 13,900; peak: about 69,400 a second. Storage a day: 1.2 billion × 0.5 KB = 600 GB; a year: about 219 TB. Implications: 69,400 writes a second at peak is beyond a single database, so the data must be spread across machines (ask engineering about partitioning by conversation); 600 GB a day is modest, so the cost question is retention and replication (three copies triple it); and old messages can move to cheaper storage.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add the read side: if each message is read by 3 recipients on average, reads are about 3 times writes.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Keeping all messages forever has a storage cost and a privacy risk; ask what retention users expect.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-02 · A requirements card</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in a requirements card (FW-C22-02) for an event-ticketing system for concerts, where a popular concert's tickets sell out in minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The first five minutes of a design round.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C22-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Users and job:</em> fans buying tickets; promoters selling them. <em>Must do:</em> show availability, hold tickets during checkout for a few minutes, take payment, issue a ticket. <em>Must not:</em> sell the same seat twice; let bots buy everything. <em>Latency and freshness:</em> availability within seconds; checkout steps under 2 seconds. <em>Availability and durability:</em> the sale must stay up during the spike; a paid ticket is never lost.</p>\n<p><em>Scale:</em> normal traffic is small; at on-sale, perhaps a million people arrive within five minutes (a very high peak factor). <em>Privacy and constraints:</em> payment data; resale rules by region. <em>Non-goals:</em> resale marketplace in version 1. <em>Success measure and guardrail:</em> tickets sold to real fans without oversell; guardrails: checkout abandonment, bot share.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a virtual waiting room as a requirement, since it shapes the whole design.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A waiting room protects the system and turns some people away; say how you decide who goes first.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-03 · The spike factor</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A ticket on-sale normally sees 50 requests a second. At the on-sale, 1,000,000 people arrive within 5 minutes, each making one request to the sale page. Compute the request rate and the ratio to normal, and say what design response it suggests.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Spike design.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C22.5 and CS-C22-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> 1,000,000 ÷ 300 seconds = about 3,300 requests a second, about 67 times the normal 50. A system sized for the average cannot absorb it, and sizing for 67× all year is wasteful. Responses: a waiting room that admits people at a rate the system can serve (for example 500 a second), static content served from a content network, elastic capacity added before the on-sale, and load shedding for non-essential features during the peak. If people are admitted at 500 a second, a million people take 2,000 seconds (33 minutes), which is a product decision about fairness and communication.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Randomize the order of those who arrive in the first minutes so that speed of clicking does not decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A waiting room trades system stability for some users' waiting.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-04 · Where to stop fanning out</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A feed system can write posts into followers' feeds at 20,000 writes a second for this job, and a post should reach all followers within 60 seconds. What is the largest follower count for which writing to every follower meets the target? What do you do above it?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The celebrity problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> In 60 seconds at 20,000 writes a second the job can write 20,000 × 60 = 1,200,000 feeds. So accounts up to about 1.2 million followers meet the target, with no margin; set the threshold lower (for example 500,000) to leave room for other posts. Above it, do not write to every follower: store the post once and merge it into followers' feeds at read time. The cost is a few extra reads for users who follow large accounts.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For very large accounts, write to the feeds of the most active followers first and let the rest read at open.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Hybrids are more complex; they bound the worst case.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-05 · Push or poll</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An app shows live order status to 5,000,000 active users. Compare polling every 30 seconds with pushing updates. Assume each user's order changes status about 4 times an hour and 3% of polls return new data. Compute the request rates and the wasted share.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A design choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C22.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Polling: 5,000,000 ÷ 30 = about 166,700 requests a second, of which 97% (about 161,700 a second) return nothing new. Pushing: 5,000,000 × 4 ÷ 3,600 = about 5,560 messages a second, each carrying a real update. Push uses about one thirtieth of the traffic and delivers updates immediately, but it requires holding connections or using the phone's push service, and handling devices that are offline. A mixed design: push when available, poll slowly (every few minutes) as a fallback.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Poll adaptively: fast while the order is out for delivery, slow otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Push reduces waste and adds connection management and delivery guarantees.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-06 · Failure-mode table for a ride request</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Build a failure-mode table (FW-C22-06) for a ride-hailing request: components are the app, the dispatch service, the location store, the driver app and the payment service. Give one failure each, what the rider sees and the degradation.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Resilience design.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C22-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> App: loses connection after requesting; rider sees &quot;reconnecting&quot; and the request status is recovered on reconnect (the request is stored server-side). Dispatch service: slow; rider sees &quot;finding a driver&quot; with a longer estimate; degrade by widening the search radius and queueing requests. Location store: stale driver positions; rider sees cars that are not there; degrade by showing a coarse estimate instead of precise car icons and labeling the age of data. Driver app: driver loses connection; rider sees the trip continuing; the system keeps the last known position and prompts the driver to reconnect; after a timeout the trip is flagged. Payment service: unavailable; rider completes the ride and the charge is deferred and retried, with the fare shown as pending, rather than blocking the ride.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a signal for each (the metric that would alert you).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Deferring payment accepts some uncollectible fares in exchange for riders not being stranded.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-07 · An abuse-case pass for live location sharing</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Do an abuse-case pass (FW-C22-08) for a feature that lets a user share their live location with friends for an hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Safety review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C22-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>A stalker or abusive partner</em> pressures someone into sharing, or shares a phone with sharing preset: harm is coercive tracking; controls are a visible indicator while sharing, a notification when sharing starts, a time limit that cannot be extended silently, one-tap stop, and safety guidance. <em>A stranger</em> tricks a user into sharing through a fake link: controls are sharing only with existing contacts, a confirmation that names the recipient, and a warning for new contacts. <em>A friend who leaks</em> the location to others: controls are no forwarding of the live view, and a note that the recipient can see it. <em>A minor sharing with an adult stranger:</em> controls are age-based defaults, no sharing with non-contacts, and parental settings where appropriate. Residual risk: coercion by someone with physical access to the phone cannot be eliminated; accept it, document it and provide support resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a &quot;safety check&quot; prompt after an hour asking whether the user wants to continue.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Each control adds friction for honest users; keep friction proportional to harm.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-08 · Consistency choices</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each data item choose strong or eventual consistency and say why: (a) a user's account balance; (b) the number of likes on a post; (c) the available seats on a flight at checkout; (d) a user's profile photo; (e) a delivery driver's current position.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Design tradeoffs.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C22-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Strong: a wrong balance can cause overdrafts or double spending. (b) Eventual: a few seconds of lag is harmless. (c) Strong at the moment of booking: two people must not get the same seat; the search list may be eventually consistent but the reservation step checks the source of truth. (d) Eventual for other viewers, but the owner should see their own change at once (read-your-own-writes, C21). (e) Eventual with a freshness label: the position is old within seconds anyway, and showing its age is honest.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (c), allow brief overselling and compensate (rebooking), if the business accepts that cost, as some airlines do.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Strong consistency costs speed and availability; reserve it for where mistakes are expensive.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-09 · Find the bottleneck</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A system's parts handle these loads: load balancer 50,000 requests a second, application service 8,000 requests a second, queue 20,000 messages a second, database writes 3,000 a second. The expected peak is 6,000 requests a second, each causing one database write. Where is the bottleneck, by how much, and what are the options?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Capacity review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C22.5 and Amdahl's law (C21, mental models, section 9).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The database writes (3,000 a second) are the tightest limit: the peak needs 6,000, twice its capacity. The application service (8,000) has 33% headroom over the 6,000 peak, and the load balancer and queue are far above. Options: put the writes on the queue and let a worker write at the database's pace (the user sees &quot;processing,&quot; and the backlog drains after the peak); batch writes; split the data across two databases so that each handles 3,000; or reduce writes per request. The queue option is cheapest if a short delay is acceptable; splitting the data fixes capacity without a delay and costs more engineering.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Cache and combine updates to the same record so fewer writes reach the database.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Asynchronous writes need idempotency and a status the user can check.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c22-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C22-10 · A timed full design</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 30-minute answer to: &quot;Design the system that shows a food-delivery customer the courier's live location and an estimated arrival time.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. State assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 45-minute budget in C22.2, compressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The spine (FW-C22-01), scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Clarify: the customer sees a map and an estimate, updated every few seconds, from order pickup to delivery; privacy (location shown only to the customer for that order, only during the delivery); battery on the courier's phone. Estimate (invented): 2 million deliveries a day, with 30 minutes of tracking each at one update per 5 seconds: 360 updates per delivery, 720 million updates a day, about 8,300 a second, and about 25,000 at a peak factor of 3; each update is small (about 100 bytes), so about 72 GB a day if kept, and only the latest position is needed live. Sketch: the courier app sends positions to a location service that keeps the latest in a fast store; the customer app receives updates by push (or polling every few seconds as a fallback); an estimate service combines position, route and traffic. Tradeoffs: update frequency against battery and cost; precision against privacy (fuzz the position near the customer's home until arrival); freshness against cost.</p>\n<p>Failure: if the courier loses signal, show the last position with its age and widen the estimate; if the estimate service fails, show the last estimate and label it. Abuse: only the customer of that order can see the courier, and for a limited time; the courier's privacy after delivery. Measures: 95th-percentile age of the displayed position, estimate error, battery impact, &quot;where is my courier&quot; contacts. Rollout: one city first.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Show the estimate and a coarse progress bar and omit the live map, to reduce cost and privacy exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A live map delights customers and costs battery, data and privacy; a progress bar is cheap and less satisfying.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: requirements and estimates.</em> Use IQ-C22-01, IQ-C22-02, EX-C22-01, EX-C22-02 and EX-C22-03. Fill a requirements card and an estimate sheet for a new prompt each day, saying the decision each number drives.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: designs and tradeoffs.</em> Use IQ-C22-03, IQ-C22-04, IQ-C22-05, IQ-C22-07, EX-C22-04, EX-C22-05 and EX-C22-08. Draw the write and read paths from memory and defend one tradeoff aloud.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: failure, abuse and communication.</em> Use IQ-C22-06, IQ-C22-08, IQ-C22-09, IQ-C22-10, EX-C22-06, EX-C22-07, EX-C22-09 and EX-C22-10. Run a full 30-minute design with a partner who changes the scale midway and asks &quot;who could abuse this?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt with &quot;Why that?&quot; and &quot;What if it fails?&quot; at random. Practice answering in one sentence tied to the user, then returning to your plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Read a real design.</strong> Read a public engineering write-up of a system you use (a notification, feed or payments design) and extract its requirements, numbers and tradeoffs in one page.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I clarify users, job, scale and constraints before drawing?</li>\n<li>Did I estimate average and peak load and storage, and name the decisions they drive?</li>\n<li>Did I sketch the write and read paths and mark where the user waits?</li>\n<li>Did I name the tradeoff and choose with a user reason?</li>\n<li>Did I say what the user sees when each part fails?</li>\n<li>Did I run an abuse-case pass where people or money are involved?</li>\n<li>Did I choose consistency and delivery guarantees by the cost of a wrong result?</li>\n<li>Did I avoid naming technology without a reason?</li>\n<li>Did I close with an executive summary and a question for engineering?</li>\n<li>Did I stay within the time?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the numbers you used, the main tradeoff, the failure and abuse cases, your score out of ten and the step you rushed.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Pick a feature your team owns and write its requirements card (FW-C22-02), including the non-functional requirements nobody wrote down (freshness, durability, consistency, privacy). Review it with the engineering lead and note what was missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Estimate its load with the sheet (FW-C22-03): average, peak, storage, and one cost figure. Compare with what engineering knows; note the biggest discrepancy and why.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Draw the write and read paths for one core action with an engineer, marking what the user waits for and what is asynchronous.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Build the failure-mode table for the feature (FW-C22-06): for each component, what the user sees when it fails. Take one gap (a blank screen, a confusing error) to design.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> If the feature touches people, location, messages or money, run an abuse-case pass (FW-C22-08) with security or trust and safety. Record the controls and the residual risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Write the design one-pager (FW-C22-07) in an engineer version and an executive version, and test each on someone from that audience.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the requirements card with the missing requirements (a time you framed a problem so a team could design well), the failure and abuse tables (a time you anticipated what could go wrong), and the two-audience one-pager (a time you communicated a technical design). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c22-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A PM-level design answer is a product decision expressed as a system: start with users, the core job, scale and constraints, state assumptions, and let numbers drive choices, not technology names.</li>\n<li>Estimate with a stated method (daily users × actions ÷ 86,400, a peak factor, item sizes and retention), say what decision each number drives, and sanity-check; peaks, not averages, size the system.</li>\n<li>Sketch a handful of components and walk a write and a read through them, marking what the user waits for; choose consistency, freshness and delivery guarantees by the cost of a wrong, stale or duplicated result.</li>\n<li>Name the tradeoff dials (latency against freshness, consistency against availability, cost, privacy against capability, push against pull) and choose with a reason tied to the user.</li>\n<li>Design for failure and misuse: say what the user sees when each part fails and how it degrades, run an abuse-case pass wherever people, location, messages or money are involved, and close with a one-page summary an engineer can act on and an executive can follow.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c22-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Martin Kleppmann, <em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> (2017), for the ideas behind consistency, queues and replication <code>[V: O'Reilly, March 2017, ISBN 9781449373320]</code>.</li>\n<li>Betsy Beyer and others (eds.), <em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> (2016), on capacity, overload and graceful degradation <code>[V: Site Reliability Engineering, O'Reilly, 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Alex Xu, <em>System Design Interview: An Insider's Guide</em>, Volume 1 (2020), as an example of how engineering interviews treat these designs; read it for the vocabulary and apply the PM-level bar from this chapter <code>[V: Volume 1, 12 June 2020, ISBN 9798664653403 (independently published; Byte Code)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Jeff Dean's widely circulated list of latency numbers for computers (slides &quot;Numbers Everyone Should Know&quot; from his talk on building large-scale distributed systems; popularized as &quot;Latency Numbers Every Programmer Should Know&quot;; the figures are about a decade old and later estimates differ) <code>[V: Dean's talk slides; later estimates differ]</code>, and Little's law in any standard queueing reference <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n<li>Luke Stone, &quot;Bringing Pokémon GO to life on Google Cloud,&quot; Google Cloud blog, 29 September 2016 (cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/bringing-pokemon-go-to-life-on-google-cloud). The source for CS-C22-02.</li>\n<li>Public engineering write-ups of notification, feed and payments systems from large technology companies, as worked examples.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c23\"></span>\n<h2>C23 — Machine Learning, Ranking and Recommendation for PMs</h2>\n<span id=\"c23-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Improve the recommendations in a music app for a user who plays the same ten songs.&quot; &quot;Search results for a class of queries got worse. How do you find out why?&quot; &quot;A new ranking raises time spent but users say they feel worse. What do you do?&quot; &quot;Choose the threshold for a fraud classifier.&quot; These questions draw on the classical machine-learning ideas behind feeds, search, recommendations and risk scores. A PM is not asked to train a model. A PM is asked to define the decision a model supports, understand what the model learns from, choose and read the right metrics, see how the system can go wrong (including through its own outputs), and design experiments that can tell whether a change helped.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: ranking, recommendation, search and classification questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and other consumer-scale and payments companies, in product, analytical and AI-focused rounds <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Questions on large language models are in C24 and C25; this chapter covers the older, still dominant ideas behind them. On the job it is the work of PMs on feeds, search, recommendations, fraud and trust-and-safety systems.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you treat a model as a means to a product outcome <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a clear decision and label; error costs that set thresholds; an understanding of stages in a ranking system; metrics that separate engagement from satisfaction and short from long term; awareness of data problems (bias, noise, cold start) and of feedback loops; and a sound experiment plan that accounts for novelty and interference.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state what the model predicts, from what data, and what decision it supports.</li>\n<li>You choose thresholds and operating points from the costs of each error type and the base rate.</li>\n<li>You describe a ranking system in stages and say where you would intervene.</li>\n<li>You choose a metric stack that separates engagement from satisfaction and short from long term, with guardrails.</li>\n<li>You name data problems and feedback loops, and design for exploration.</li>\n<li>You plan experiments on ranking systems with their pitfalls: novelty, interference and slow effects.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end ranking answers: &quot;use machine learning&quot; with no decision or label, accuracy quoted for a rare event, a single click metric, ignoring cold start and feedback loops, an offline improvement declared a product win, and no view of what the system does to the people and creators who feed it.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-1-why-ml-literacy-matters-for-pms-and-what-interviewers-\"></span>\n<h4>C23.1 Why ML literacy matters for PMs, and what interviewers test</h4>\n<p>Many product surfaces are, underneath, predictions: which items to show, which messages are spam, which payments are fraud, how long a delivery will take. A PM who understands the shape of those predictions can set the right goal, ask engineers the right questions, interpret results correctly and notice when a metric improved for the wrong reason. A PM who does not will accept &quot;the model is better&quot; without asking better at what, measured how, on whom, and at what cost.</p>\n<p><strong>What this chapter is not.</strong> It does not teach how to build models. It teaches the product-level concepts: what a prediction needs, how to judge it, how ranking systems are staged, how they fail, and how to test changes. The next chapters build on it for language models (C24) and for building and evaluating AI products (C25).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how to improve a feed, a candidate says: &quot;Use a deep learning model trained on engagement to rank posts.&quot; The interviewer asks what the model should predict, for whom, and whether more engagement is the goal. The candidate has treated &quot;machine learning&quot; as the answer and has not said what decision it serves or what could go wrong when the system optimizes clicks. A better opening: &quot;First, what is the feed for? If it is to help people see things they value, clicks are only a rough proxy, so I'd define what we want the model to predict and how we'd check that it matches what people value.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Proxy worship:</em> optimizing what is easy to measure (clicks) can diverge from what matters (satisfaction), as in the Netflix Prize case (CS-C23-02). <em>Treating the model as the product:</em> data, thresholds, interface and incentives often matter more than the algorithm.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-2-prediction-in-plain-terms-features-labels-training-inf\"></span>\n<h4>C23.2 Prediction in plain terms: features, labels, training, inference, evaluation</h4>\n<p>A <strong>model</strong> is a function that has been fitted to data to predict something about new cases. Its parts (FW-C23-01):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Training data</strong> is the set of past examples a model learns from.</li>\n<li>A <strong>label</strong> is the outcome the model is trying to predict for each training example, such as &quot;this email was marked as spam,&quot; &quot;this video was watched to the end,&quot; or &quot;this payment was later disputed as fraud.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Features</em> are the inputs the model uses to predict: attributes of the user, the item and the context (time of day, device, past behavior).</li>\n<li><em>Training</em> is fitting the model to the examples; <em>inference</em> is using the trained model on a new case to produce a prediction (a score).</li>\n<li><em>Evaluation</em> checks how well the model predicts on examples it did not train on (held-out data), which estimates how it will do on the future.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Overfitting.</strong> <strong>Overfitting</strong> is a model learning the quirks of its training data so closely that it predicts new cases poorly. A model that scores 99% on the data it trained on and 80% on held-out data has overfitted. The PM-level consequence: always ask for performance on data the model has not seen, and on data that looks like the future (recent, and from the segments that matter).</p>\n<p><strong>The PM's questions.</strong> What decision does the prediction support, and who acts on it? What is the label, and does it really measure what we care about? (A label such as &quot;clicked&quot; measures interest in a headline, not value from the article.) What data do we have, from whom, and what is missing? What does each error cost? How will we know the model is working after launch, and what happens when the world changes (a model trained on last year's behavior may decay)? This is the prediction brief.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use a model.</strong> If a simple rule does the job and is easier to explain, audit and change, use it: &quot;hold a payment above $10,000 from a new account&quot; is a rule; a model earns its place when patterns are too many and subtle for rules. A model also needs enough labeled data and a way to learn whether it is right.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-3-classification-and-thresholds-precision-recall-and-the\"></span>\n<h4>C23.3 Classification and thresholds: precision, recall, and the cost of each error type</h4>\n<p>Many decisions are yes or no: is this message spam, is this payment fraud, is this post harmful? A <em>classifier</em> produces a score, and a <strong>threshold</strong> is the score above which the system acts. Moving the threshold trades one kind of error for another.</p>\n<p><strong>Errors.</strong> A <em>false positive</em> is flagging something that is fine; a <em>false negative</em> is missing something that is bad. <strong>Precision and recall</strong> are two measures of a classifier: precision is the share of flagged items that are correct, and recall is the share of all true items that were flagged; raising one often lowers the other. Precision answers &quot;when we flag, how often are we right?&quot; Recall answers &quot;of all the bad things, how many did we catch?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Base rates.</strong> When the thing you are looking for is rare, even an accurate classifier flags mostly good items (C12). Take 1,000,000 items a day with 1% bad: 10,000 bad and 990,000 good. A classifier with 80% recall and a 0.8% false-positive rate catches 8,000 and wrongly flags about 7,900: a precision of about 50%. If only 0.1% are bad (1,000 bad), the same classifier catches 800 and wrongly flags about 8,000: a precision of about 9%. The classifier did not change; the base rate did. Accuracy is misleading for rare events: a model that never flags anything is 99% accurate when 1% are bad, and catches nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Choosing the threshold by cost (FW-C23-02).</strong> Assign a cost to each error (a wrongly blocked good item costs a review and a frustrated user; a missed bad item costs harm and cleanup), compute total cost at several thresholds, and add practical limits (how many flags humans can review in a day). Worked example 1 does this with numbers. The right threshold is not the one with the highest accuracy; it is the one that minimizes the cost you care about within your constraints. Often the best design has more than two outcomes: act automatically on very high scores, send a middle band to human review, and allow the rest (Stripe-style fraud design, IQ-C23-09).</p>\n<p><strong>Calibration.</strong> A score is <em>calibrated</em> if items scored 0.8 are really bad about 80% of the time. Calibrated scores let you set thresholds by cost and combine them with other information; uncalibrated scores only rank. Ask whether scores are calibrated if you plan to use them as probabilities.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Cost-based thresholds make the tradeoff explicit and defensible. <strong>When not to optimize one number.</strong> When the costs of errors differ by group or context (a false accusation against a person, a safety risk), treat the thresholds as policy decisions with review, not as tuning.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-4-ranking-and-recommendation-candidate-generation-rankin\"></span>\n<h4>C23.4 Ranking and recommendation: candidate generation, ranking, re-ranking, feedback</h4>\n<p>A <strong>recommender system</strong> is a system that predicts which items a user will value and ranks them for display. <strong>Ranking</strong> is ordering items by a predicted score so that the most valuable appear first. Real systems have far too many items to score each one for every request, so they work in stages (FW-C23-03):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Candidate generation.</strong> <strong>Candidate generation</strong> is the cheap first stage that narrows a huge catalog to a manageable set of plausible items. Methods include items similar to what the user liked, items from accounts they follow, what is trending, and nearest neighbors in an embedding space. An <strong>embedding</strong> is a list of numbers that represents an item or user so that similar things lie close together; it lets a system find &quot;items like this one&quot; quickly. The aim is recall: do not miss good items. A catalog of 100,000,000 videos might yield about 1,000 candidates.</li>\n<li><strong>Ranking.</strong> A heavier model scores each candidate using many features (the user's history, the item's quality, the context) to predict value, and orders them. It might predict several things: the chance of a click, of watching to the end, of a like, of a &quot;not interested&quot; signal, and combine them into one score. The combination weights are product decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-ranking.</strong> Rules and adjustments are applied to the ranked list: diversity (not ten videos from one creator), policy (remove harmful items), freshness, business needs, and positions for exploration.</li>\n<li><strong>Presentation.</strong> Layout, number of items, explanations (&quot;because you watched...&quot;), and what happens when the user interacts.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Signals.</strong> <em>Collaborative</em> signals come from the behavior of many users (&quot;people who liked this also liked that&quot;); <em>content-based</em> signals come from item attributes (genre, topic, language). Collaborative methods are powerful and need history; content-based methods work for new items. <strong>Cold start</strong> (C19's cold-start problem, applied to recommendations) is the difficulty for new users and new items, who lack history; defenses are content-based features, popularity within a segment, asking the user a few preferences, and deliberately showing new items to learn (C23.8).</p>\n<p><strong>The latency budget (C21.2).</strong> All of this runs while the user waits, within a few hundred milliseconds. Scoring cost is the scale limit: scoring 1,000 candidates per request at 20,000 requests a second is 20 million scores a second; at 0.1 millisecond per score that is 2,000 processor-seconds a second, about 2,000 cores; halving candidates halves the cost (Worked example 2). This is why stages exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Where a PM intervenes.</strong> Better candidates (new sources, languages, fresher items), better labels and objectives (what is the ranking trying to predict?), re-ranking rules (diversity, safety, creator fairness), and presentation (explanations, controls). Each has a different cost, risk and testability.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-5-search-and-relevance-at-pm-depth-query-understanding-r\"></span>\n<h4>C23.5 Search and relevance at PM depth: query understanding, ranking signals, quality measures</h4>\n<p>Search is a recommender where the user states an intent. A search system (FW-C23-04) understands the query (spelling, synonyms, language, what kind of answer is wanted), <em>retrieves</em> candidate results from an index, <em>ranks</em> them using signals, and presents them.</p>\n<p><strong>Kinds of query.</strong> <em>Navigational</em> (&quot;bank login&quot;): one right answer, and getting it wrong is costly. <em>Informational</em> (&quot;why is the sky blue&quot;): many acceptable answers, quality and trust matter. <em>Transactional</em> (&quot;buy running shoes&quot;): relevance plus availability, price and trust. <em>Ambiguous</em> (&quot;jaguar&quot;): may need diversity or a clarifying prompt. A quality problem often hides in one class, so segment by query type before judging.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranking signals.</strong> The words and their match to the item's content; the item's quality and authority; freshness; the user's context (location, language, history); and interaction signals (what people click and stay on). Each signal can be gamed or biased, so ask what it really measures.</p>\n<p><strong>Measuring quality.</strong> Human raters judge result relevance on a scale (for example 0 to 3) using written guidelines; the product then computes measures such as <em>precision at k</em> (the share of the top k results judged relevant) and <em>NDCG</em> (normalized discounted cumulative gain), which gives more credit for relevant results placed higher: a result's gain is divided by the logarithm of its position, then compared with the best possible ordering. Worked example 4 shows two rankings with equal precision at 5 and different NDCG. Behavior measures complement raters: the share of searches with a click, the share where the user reformulates the query (a sign of failure), the time to a successful result, and abandonment. Raters see quality but not real intent; behavior sees intent but is noisy and biased by position. Use both.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Search and recommendation quality depends on language coverage and on how people actually type. Users often mix languages in one query and write local languages in the Latin alphabet (transliteration), and training data and quality raters are scarcer for some languages <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's synthesis; no source checked]</code>. The consequence for a PM: segment quality measures by language and script, build rater guidelines and test sets for each, and expect cold start to be harsher where data is thin (fall back to content-based signals and popularity within the language). Check current coverage and published language support before assuming parity.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-6-data-quality-bias-label-noise-cold-start\"></span>\n<h4>C23.6 Data: quality, bias, label noise, cold start</h4>\n<p>A model reflects its data. Problems to look for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Selection and exposure bias.</strong> The data contains only what was shown and acted on. If the old system never showed a type of item, the data has no evidence about it, and the model will never learn that people like it (a form of survivorship bias, C12).</li>\n<li><strong>Position bias.</strong> <strong>Position bias</strong> is the tendency of items shown higher to be clicked more regardless of quality. If the model learns from clicks without correcting for it, it learns that &quot;top positions are good&quot; and reinforces the current order. A common remedy is to randomize positions in a small slice of traffic to measure how much position alone matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Label noise and proxy labels.</strong> Labels may be wrong (human raters disagree, fraud labels arrive weeks late and only for disputed payments) or may measure something other than what you want (a click is not satisfaction). Ask how the label is produced, how late and how noisy it is, and who is missing.</li>\n<li><strong>Implicit and explicit feedback.</strong> Implicit feedback (clicks, watch time, skips) is plentiful and ambiguous; explicit feedback (ratings, likes, &quot;not interested&quot;) is clearer and sparse and biased toward people who give it. Combine them; treat &quot;not interested&quot; as a strong signal.</li>\n<li><strong>Drift.</strong> The world changes: tastes, language, tactics of fraudsters. A model decays; monitor it and retrain on fresh data.</li>\n<li><strong>Privacy and consent.</strong> What may be collected and used (C21.8), and what must be deleted on request.</li>\n<li><strong>Cold start.</strong> New users and items have no history; handle them explicitly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ask:</strong> where did this data come from, what does it leave out, and whom does that hurt?</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-7-metrics-for-ranked-systems-engagement-versus-satisfact\"></span>\n<h4>C23.7 Metrics for ranked systems: engagement versus satisfaction, short versus long term, diversity, fairness</h4>\n<p>A ranked system needs a stack of metrics, not one (FW-C23-05; C05 gave the general method).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>What it tells you</th>\n<th>Danger if alone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engagement (immediate)</td>\n<td>Clicks, plays, time spent</td>\n<td>What attracts attention</td>\n<td>Rewards clickbait and compulsion (Goodhart, C05)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Satisfaction</td>\n<td>Completion, saves, shares, survey (&quot;did you enjoy this?&quot;), low &quot;not interested&quot; rate</td>\n<td>Whether people valued it</td>\n<td>Surveys are sparse and slow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-term</td>\n<td>Retention, return frequency, subscription renewal</td>\n<td>Whether the product keeps its value</td>\n<td>Slow; hard to attribute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diversity and discovery</td>\n<td>Variety of creators and topics, share of new items shown</td>\n<td>Health of the catalog and the user's horizon</td>\n<td>May cost short-term engagement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fairness and creator health</td>\n<td>Exposure by group, new-creator success</td>\n<td>Whether the ecosystem is sustainable and equitable</td>\n<td>Needs definitions that fit the context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guardrails</td>\n<td>Reports, complaints, harmful-content rate, latency</td>\n<td>Harm and cost</td>\n<td>Not a goal in itself; a limit on the others</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Short against long term.</strong> A change that raises clicks this week may lower retention in a month, because people who feel manipulated leave. Evaluate on long-term measures through a long-running holdout (C14) and tie the primary objective to something people would endorse on reflection. <strong>Choosing the objective.</strong> What the model predicts becomes what the system optimizes: predicting clicks yields clicky results; predicting &quot;watched most of it and did not regret it&quot; yields different ones. Combining predictions with weights is a product decision to be made explicitly and revisited.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-8-feedback-loops-and-unintended-effects-popularity-bias-\"></span>\n<h4>C23.8 Feedback loops and unintended effects: popularity bias, filter bubbles, creator incentives</h4>\n<p>A <strong>feedback loop</strong> is a cycle in which a system's outputs become its future inputs, so that its own choices shape the data it learns from. In a recommender, what is shown gets clicked, clicks train the model, and the model shows more of the same. Effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Popularity bias.</strong> <strong>Popularity bias</strong> is a tendency for a recommender to favor already-popular items because they have the most data and the most past clicks, which starves new and niche items of exposure and makes the popular more popular (CS-C23-01).</li>\n<li><strong>Filter bubbles and narrowing.</strong> Personalization can shrink what a user sees to what they already like, reducing discovery and, for some content, exposure to varied views. The research on how strong this effect is, and for whom, is mixed <code>[V: Nguyen, Hui, Harper, Terveen and Konstan, Exploring the Filter Bubble, WWW 2014, pp. 677 to 686: slightly narrowing over time, less narrowing for users who took recommendations; later work disputes the mechanism]</code>; the PM-level point is to measure variety and not assume it.</li>\n<li><strong>Creator incentives.</strong> Creators adapt to what the system rewards: if clicks win, headlines get more sensational; if watch time wins, videos get longer. The ranking is a policy that shapes supply. Ask what behavior this ranking invites.</li>\n<li><strong>Metric gaming and abuse.</strong> Bad actors learn the signals and manipulate them (fake engagement, spam).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exploration.</strong> <strong>Exploration</strong> is deliberately showing items whose value the system is unsure of, in order to learn, at some cost to immediate engagement, as opposed to <em>exploiting</em> what it already believes is best. A small, controlled exploration budget (for example a few percent of slots) lets new items and new tastes be evaluated. Worked example 5 shows that learning about a new item's click rate takes about a thousand impressions, and that a budget for it is a tiny share of total traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Monitoring the loop (FW-C23-06).</strong> Track concentration (share of exposure going to the top items), exposure for new items and creators, variety per user, and long-term retention; and review what the ranking rewards.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-9-experimenting-on-ranking-systems\"></span>\n<h4>C23.9 Experimenting on ranking systems</h4>\n<p>C14 gave the foundations. Ranking systems add specific problems (FW-C23-07).</p>\n<p><strong>Offline against online.</strong> <strong>Offline evaluation</strong> is judging a model on historical data without showing it to users. It is fast and cheap and can mislead: it measures how well the model predicts past behavior under the old ranking, which carries the old system's biases, and cannot show how users react to new results, as the Netflix Prize showed (CS-C23-02). Use offline evaluation as a gate (is this worth testing?) and online experiments as the judge.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfalls.</strong> <em>Novelty effects:</em> a new ranking draws curiosity that fades; read effects by week (C14.4). <em>Slow effects:</em> retention and creator behavior change over weeks; use a long-term holdout. <em>Interference:</em> users affect each other (in a feed, the content one person posts is seen by others; in a marketplace, a buyer's purchase removes an item), so randomizing users may not isolate the effect; consider cluster randomization or running the test on one side of a market and measuring the other. <em>Position effects:</em> when comparing rankings, the difference in positions confounds clicks. <em>Multiple metrics:</em> with many metrics some will move by chance (C12.7); fix primary and guardrail metrics in advance (C14.5).</p>\n<p><strong>Counterfactual data.</strong> To evaluate new rankings from logs, log what was shown and with what probability (including a little randomization), so that you can estimate what would have happened under another policy. Without such logging, you can only guess.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision rule.</strong> Write it before the test: the primary metric's threshold, guardrail margins, the required duration, and what you will do if results are mixed (C17.4).</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-10-answer-architecture-for-improve-the-recommendations-s\"></span>\n<h4>C23.10 Answer architecture for &quot;improve the recommendations, search or feed&quot; questions</h4>\n<p>Section 4 gives the full architecture. In outline: state the goal and who it is for; diagnose the current system by stage (candidates, ranking, re-ranking, presentation) and by segment; propose options at the stage where the problem is; choose metrics with guardrails and a time horizon; plan the experiment; and name the feedback loops and the risks.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-c23-11-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h4>C23.11 Worked examples</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked examples: choosing a classifier threshold by cost, the stages of a recommendation funnel with a cost estimate, reading a ranking experiment with mixed metrics, comparing search rankings with precision and NDCG, and an exploration budget for new items. Section 11 adds ten questions with model answers and follow-ups.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The metric card (FW-C05-01), the experiment decision-rule table (FW-C14-05), the conflicting-metrics readout (FW-C14-07), the launch-or-hold table (FW-C17-04), the latency budget (FW-C21-03), the capacity sheet (FW-C22-03) and the abuse-case pass (FW-C22-08) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-01 · The prediction brief · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product decision that a prediction could support.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the decision and who acts on it; the label and how it is produced; the data and what it leaves out; the cost of each error; the threshold or ranking use; evaluation on unseen, recent and segment data; monitoring and retraining.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> a rule would not do the job as well.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a model captures subtle patterns and is harder to explain, audit and change.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> no decision; a proxy label taken as truth; evaluation only on training data; no monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 and IQ-C23-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision and actor</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label (source, delay, noise)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data and gaps</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost of a false positive and a false negative</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation data</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring and retraining</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-02 · Threshold by cost · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> classifier scores, a base rate, costs of each error and a capacity limit for review.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table at several thresholds (flagged, true and false positives, false negatives, precision, recall, total cost), the choice within capacity, and a view of how the base rate changes precision.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> costs can be estimated and revisited.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a lower threshold catches more and costs more review and more wrong flags.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> maximizing accuracy; ignoring capacity; ignoring the base rate; one threshold for all contexts.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>precision = true positives ÷ flagged; recall = true positives ÷ all true; cost = false positives × cost₁ + false negatives × cost₂.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-03 · The recommendation funnel · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a catalog, a user context and a latency budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the stages (candidate generation, ranking, re-ranking, presentation) with, for each, its goal, its signals, its failure modes, its latency share and its cost, and where to intervene.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> scoring everything is too expensive.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> more candidates raise recall and cost; heavier ranking raises quality and latency.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> candidates that miss good items; ranking objectives that drift from value; re-ranking rules that no one reviews.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Goal</th>\n<th>Typical size</th>\n<th>Failure</th>\n<th>Intervention</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Candidate generation</td>\n<td>Do not miss good items</td>\n<td>100,000,000 to 1,000</td>\n<td>Missing new or niche items</td>\n<td>New sources, fresher items</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranking</td>\n<td>Order by predicted value</td>\n<td>1,000 to 100</td>\n<td>Optimizing a proxy</td>\n<td>Better labels and objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Re-ranking</td>\n<td>Diversity, safety, freshness</td>\n<td>100 to 20</td>\n<td>Rules that no one reviews</td>\n<td>Explicit policy and tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Presentation</td>\n<td>Make results usable</td>\n<td>20 shown</td>\n<td>Confusing layout</td>\n<td>Explanations, controls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-04 · Search relevance diagnosis · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a complaint or a metric drop in search quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the affected query class, the stage at fault (understanding, retrieval, ranking, presentation), measures from raters and behavior, and the fix with its test.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> queries can be segmented and rated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> raters are accurate and slow; behavior is fast and noisy.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> judging overall quality only; ignoring language and query type; trusting clicks with position bias.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and IQ-C23-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Segment by query type and language → Compare rater scores and behavior → Locate the stage → Fix and test → Monitor the class.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-05 · The ranked-system metric stack · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a ranking system and its goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> metrics by layer (engagement, satisfaction, long-term, diversity and fairness, guardrails), each with its time horizon and its danger when used alone, and the primary metric chosen.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the objective can be tied to what people value on reflection.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a long-term metric is trustworthy and slow.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a single engagement metric; no long-term holdout; no guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3 and IQ-C23-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-06 · The feedback-loop check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a system whose outputs shape its training data.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> where outputs become inputs; popularity and concentration measures; exposure for new items and creators; an exploration budget; creator-incentive review; monitoring and a response plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> exposure and outcomes are logged.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> exploration costs short-term engagement.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> training on clicks from the system's own ranking without correction; no measure of concentration.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5 and CS-C23-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-07 · The experiment plan for ranking · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a ranking change.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an offline gate, the online design (unit, duration, interference handling, position handling), primary and guardrail metrics with margins, a novelty read by week, a long-term holdout, logging for counterfactuals, and the decision rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the platform can randomize and log.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> longer tests delay launch and give truer answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> declaring victory from offline metrics; reading week one; ignoring the creator side.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3 and IQ-C23-10.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c23-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C23-08 · The &quot;improve it&quot; answer spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a prompt to improve recommendations, search or a feed.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer in seven steps: goal and user; diagnosis by stage and segment; options at the failing stage; metric stack and guardrails; experiment; feedback loops and risks; decision and what would change it.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can ask clarifying questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure shows judgment and can slow the best idea.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> &quot;use ML&quot; with no diagnosis; one metric; no loops.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c23-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c23-the-improve-the-recommendations-search-or-feed-answer-for-a-\"></span>\n<h4>The &quot;improve the recommendations, search or feed&quot; answer, for a 30-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Goal and user</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>What the surface is for, whom it serves, what success looks like for them and the company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnose</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The current system by stage (candidates, ranking, re-ranking, presentation) and by segment; what data says</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Two or three changes at the failing stage, with cost and risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The metric stack: primary, satisfaction, long term, diversity, guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experiment</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Offline gate, online test, novelty and interference, holdout, decision rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loops and risks</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Feedback loops, creator incentives, cold start, fairness; what would change the plan</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer names a specific problem (the same ten songs, a drop in search quality), spend the time on diagnosis and options. If you run behind, keep the goal, the stage you would change, the metric stack and the experiment.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> State the decision a model supports and what it predicts: &quot;The ranking predicts whether someone will watch most of a video; I'd question whether that is what we want.&quot; Name the cost of each error before choosing a threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd change [stage] to [option] because [diagnosis], judge it on [primary metric] with guardrails on [guardrails], and test it for [weeks] with a long-term holdout. The main risk is [feedback loop or incentive], which I'd monitor with [measure].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c23-type-improve-recommendations\"></span>\n<h4>Type: improve recommendations</h4>\n<p>Diagnose by stage; consider exploration and cold start; choose satisfaction and long-term measures beyond clicks.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-type-search-quality\"></span>\n<h4>Type: search quality</h4>\n<p>Segment by query type and language; use raters and behavior; locate the stage.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-type-classifier-or-risk-score-fraud-spam-harm\"></span>\n<h4>Type: classifier or risk score (fraud, spam, harm)</h4>\n<p>Use the prediction brief and cost-based thresholds; consider a three-zone design with human review.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-type-a-ranking-change-shows-mixed-metrics\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a ranking change shows mixed metrics</h4>\n<p>Use the metric stack and the decision table; read effects by week; check guardrails and the long-term holdout.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-type-fairness-or-bias-in-a-ranked-system\"></span>\n<h4>Type: fairness or bias in a ranked system</h4>\n<p>Define the exposure or selection rate by group; test for gaps; consider the feedback loop; involve policy and legal.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What does the model predict?&quot;</td>\n<td>Label and objective</td>\n<td>Name the label and whether it matches the goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The model is 99% accurate.&quot;</td>\n<td>Base rate</td>\n<td>Ask the base rate; give precision and recall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Clicks went up.&quot;</td>\n<td>Proxy</td>\n<td>Ask about satisfaction and long-term retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why not more personalization?&quot;</td>\n<td>Loops</td>\n<td>Mention narrowing, concentration and exploration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you test it?&quot;</td>\n<td>Experiment plan</td>\n<td>Offline gate, online test, novelty, holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;New items get no traffic.&quot;</td>\n<td>Cold start</td>\n<td>Content-based signals and an exploration budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Is it fair?&quot;</td>\n<td>Group effects</td>\n<td>Define the measure by group and test it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c23-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption (invented). The arithmetic was computed. Each is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-worked-example-1-choosing-a-classifier-threshold-by-cost-ill\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Choosing a classifier threshold by cost (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A platform's classifier scores 1,000,000 items a day for abuse. One percent are truly abusive: 10,000 bad and 990,000 good (invented). Reviewers can handle 20,000 flagged items a day. The team debates thresholds.</p>\n<p><strong>Costs (FW-C23-02).</strong> A wrongly flagged good item costs $2 (a review and some user frustration). A missed abusive item costs $50 (harm, cleanup, complaints).</p>\n<p><strong>Three thresholds.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Recall</th>\n<th>Precision</th>\n<th>Flagged</th>\n<th>True positives</th>\n<th>False positives</th>\n<th>False negatives</th>\n<th>Total cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strict</td>\n<td>60%</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>7,500</td>\n<td>6,000</td>\n<td>1,500</td>\n<td>4,000</td>\n<td>1,500 × $2 + 4,000 × $50 = $203,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>16,000</td>\n<td>8,000</td>\n<td>8,000</td>\n<td>2,000</td>\n<td>8,000 × $2 + 2,000 × $50 = $116,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loose</td>\n<td>95%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>47,500</td>\n<td>9,500</td>\n<td>38,000</td>\n<td>500</td>\n<td>38,000 × $2 + 500 × $50 = $101,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Reading it.</strong> On cost alone, the loose threshold is cheapest ($101,000). But it flags 47,500 items a day and reviewers can handle 20,000; the medium threshold flags 16,000, which fits. Within the capacity limit the medium threshold is the best choice at $116,000, and it is far better than strict ($203,000), which misses 4,000 abusive items. The loose threshold would be feasible only with more reviewers: handling the extra 27,500 reviews a day costs staff, and the cost model would have to include that.</p>\n<p><strong>A better design than one threshold.</strong> Act automatically on the very highest scores (which have high precision), send a middle band to human review, and leave the rest. This raises the value of each reviewer hour.</p>\n<p><strong>The base rate trap.</strong> The medium threshold wrongly flags 8,000 of 990,000 good items, a false-positive rate of 0.81%. If abuse were rarer, say 0.1% (1,000 bad, 999,000 good), the same classifier would catch 800 and wrongly flag about 8,070: a precision of 9%. Nothing about the classifier changed. The queue would be 90% good items, so the cost of wasted reviews per true catch would rise tenfold, from about $2 to about $20. Always ask the base rate before accepting a precision number.</p>\n<p><strong>What I'd say.</strong> &quot;We'd pick the medium threshold because it minimizes cost within review capacity, and revisit it each quarter as the base rate, the costs and the classifier change. For the top-scoring items we'd act automatically, and I'd measure precision on the automatic band separately.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The threshold came from costs and capacity, not from accuracy, and the base rate explained why a precision figure can collapse.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-worked-example-2-the-stages-of-a-homepage-recommendation-ill\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — The stages of a homepage recommendation (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a streaming service of the kind Netflix operates. No fact about any real system is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A member opens the homepage. The catalog has 15,000 titles (invented). The system must produce rows of titles, personalized, in about 200 milliseconds at the 95th percentile.</p>\n<p><strong>The funnel (FW-C23-03).</strong> <em>Eligibility:</em> remove titles not licensed in the member's country or not suitable for the profile's maturity setting: about 9,000 titles remain. <em>Candidate generation:</em> several cheap sources each propose titles: continuing a series the member started, similar to recent watches (embedding neighbors), popular in their country, new releases in their favorite genres, and titles liked by similar members: together about 400 candidates. <em>Ranking:</em> a heavier model scores the 400, predicting the chance the member will start and finish each; it orders them. <em>Re-ranking and row assembly:</em> rows are built (&quot;Continue watching,&quot; &quot;Because you watched...,&quot; &quot;New this week&quot;), with rules: do not repeat a title across rows, mix genres, give new titles a few slots, remove what the member already finished. <em>Presentation:</em> artwork, row order, and the number of titles shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Latency budget (95th percentile).</strong> Fetch features 30 ms; candidate generation 40 ms; ranking 70 ms; re-ranking and assembly 30 ms; rendering and transfer 20 ms; margin 10 ms: 200 ms in all.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the stages exist: the cost arithmetic.</strong> Suppose 5,000 homepage requests a second at peak and a ranking model that takes 0.2 ms per title. Scoring all 9,000 eligible titles needs 9,000 × 5,000 = 45 million scores a second, at 0.2 ms each: 9,000 processor-seconds a second, about 9,000 cores. Scoring 400 candidates needs 400 × 5,000 = 2 million scores a second: 400 cores, 22 times less. Candidate generation is what makes the heavy model affordable.</p>\n";
